WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.421 --> 00:00:18.188
In 1874 around 3,500 curious people paid one franc to attend an Impressionist art exhibition in Paris.

2
00:00:19.809 --> 00:00:27.415
At this exhibition an unknown artist named Claude Monet showed his painting Impression, Sunrise to the public for

3
00:00:27.455 --> 00:00:28.176
the first time.

4
00:00:29.684 --> 00:00:36.248
Monet's painting was a scene of a harbor at sunrise, with noticeable brushstrokes swirling around the canvas in

5
00:00:36.308 --> 00:00:37.128
hazy colors.

6
00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:48.475
Other now-famous artists—Edgar Degas, Pierre-Auguste Renoir, and Berthe Morisot—also showed works at this exhibition.

7
00:00:49.455 --> 00:00:51.917
And how did the public react to their paintings?

8
00:00:52.777 --> 00:00:53.578
They laughed!

9
00:00:55.679 --> 00:01:00.863
Since the 1600s the most important art exhibition in France had been the Salon.

10
00:01:01.904 --> 00:01:07.949
Every year artists submitted works to a jury of critics who selected the best paintings to exhibit.

11
00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.553
The Salon's decisions could make or break an artist's career.

12
00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:20.238
Acceptance meant publicity and opportunities for more work, but getting accepted was not easy.

13
00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.042
The Salon had strict preferences.

14
00:01:25.162 --> 00:01:26.824
Paintings had to look realistic.

15
00:01:28.724 --> 00:01:31.506
Religious and historical themes were appreciated.

16
00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:38.612
Landscapes, still lifes, and contemporary scenes were not.

17
00:01:41.915 --> 00:01:49.521
Before the Impressionists most painters focused on the Salon's preferred subjects, capturing them in paintings with dark colors.

18
00:01:50.962 --> 00:01:58.048
The painting techniques used by traditional artists hadn't changed much since the Renaissance over four hundred years earlier.

19
00:01:59.124 --> 00:02:06.046
The world, however, was beginning to change in dramatic ways, which made the late nineteenth century an exciting

20
00:02:06.106 --> 00:02:07.146
time for artists.

21
00:02:08.587 --> 00:02:14.288
Painters could buy mass-produced paint in tubes, which was far more convenient than making their own.

22
00:02:15.588 --> 00:02:21.070
Japan began trading with Europe, which introduced new styles of art to the Western world.

23
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:28.412
The city of Paris had been enlarged and modernized, and some artists were eager to modernize art as

24
00:02:28.472 --> 00:02:36.123
well. However, one modern technology represented a growing challenge for artists: photography.

25
00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:42.906
Photographs had become widespread and could perfectly capture reality.

26
00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:46.748
Did anyone need paintings anymore?

27
00:02:48.448 --> 00:02:56.572
The Impressionists believed they could compete with black-and-white photography by using their own unique tools—light and color.

28
00:02:59.952 --> 00:03:02.393
Most artists painted indoors in a studio.

29
00:03:03.674 --> 00:03:09.336
The Impressionists painted outdoors in the open air, or in the cafes and neighborhoods of the city.

30
00:03:12.137 --> 00:03:19.300
With quick, spontaneous brushstrokes, the Impressionists captured colorful impressions of everyday life as they saw them.

31
00:03:20.961 --> 00:03:28.704
Painting realistically didn't matter to the Impressionists, but it still mattered to the Salon, which rejected the Impressionists'

32
00:03:28.764 --> 00:03:36.933
work. In 1863 the Salon rejected so many paintings that it became a public controversy.

33
00:03:37.713 --> 00:03:40.555
Public controversy could be dangerous in France.

34
00:03:42.597 --> 00:03:47.560
The angry public had removed kings, politicians, and an emperor before.

35
00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:52.003
Emperor Napoleon III did not want that to happen to him.

36
00:03:53.464 --> 00:03:58.548
He decided that the rejected works should be displayed to the public in a separate exhibition.

37
00:04:00.028 --> 00:04:04.992
It was called the Salon of the Refused, and it attracted a lot of attention.

38
00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:11.036
In fact, more people attended the Salon of the Refused than the Paris Salon!

39
00:04:12.717 --> 00:04:16.479
However, few people took the rejected paintings seriously.

40
00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:20.682
The Impressionists were not discouraged by the poor reception.

41
00:04:21.543 --> 00:04:27.247
Instead they were delighted—the Salon of the Refused made the public aware of their work!

42
00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:33.203
The following year the Impressionists requested that another Salon of the Refused be held.

43
00:04:34.083 --> 00:04:36.705
But the French government refused their request.

44
00:04:38.487 --> 00:04:41.489
Further requests were also denied for the next decade.

45
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:49.334
Impatient and determined, the Impressionists decided to hold their own exhibition in 1874.

46
00:04:50.635 --> 00:04:55.719
Calling themselves the Anonymous Society of Artists, the Impressionists displayed 165 works.

47
00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:03.065
Monet, Renoir, Degas, and Morisot all participated.

48
00:05:05.026 --> 00:05:07.547
Time had not changed public opinion though.

49
00:05:08.927 --> 00:05:15.809
Upon seeing Impression, Sunrise, one critic wrote that it looked less finished than sketches for wallpaper.

50
00:05:16.769 --> 00:05:20.910
Meanwhile the Impressionists did not give up on the Paris Salon.

51
00:05:21.851 --> 00:05:26.332
They continued to submit their work, and they continued to be rejected.

52
00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:34.783
Between 1874 and 1886, the Impressionists held a total of eight independent exhibitions.

53
00:05:36.103 --> 00:05:41.646
By their third in 1877, they were officially calling themselves Impressionists.

54
00:05:43.106 --> 00:05:51.429
This show included Renoir's Ball at the Galette Windmill, a now-famous painting that sold for $78 million in

55
00:05:51.570 --> 00:06:00.725
1990. With each exhibition different artists displayed works, and something began to change: public opinion.

56
00:06:01.445 --> 00:06:05.446
People were gradually opening their eyes to the beauty of Impressionist art.

57
00:06:06.886 --> 00:06:14.468
Even the Salon began accepting the Impressionists' work, and artists like Monet finally achieved mainstream success.

58
00:06:15.749 --> 00:06:21.170
Impressionism itself evolved as newer artists experimented with new techniques.

59
00:06:22.811 --> 00:06:28.676
Small, delicate brushstrokes were replaced with bold shapes by Paul Cezanne and Paul Gauguin.

60
00:06:32.337 --> 00:06:36.218
Georges-Pierre Seurat painted scenes made of tiny dots of paint.

61
00:06:39.059 --> 00:06:42.880
These painters eventually became known as Post-Impressionists.

62
00:06:44.221 --> 00:06:50.702
Later artists like Vincent van Gogh continued to experiment and develop their own unique styles.

63
00:06:52.243 --> 00:07:00.439
Today the Impressionists are remembered as genius artists, respected as deeply as the realistic artists they rebelled against.

64
00:07:01.761 --> 00:07:04.123
Their paintings sell for millions of dollars.

65
00:07:06.364 --> 00:07:12.330
Just like in 1874, the Impressionists can draw large crowds to museums and galleries.

66
00:07:13.631 --> 00:07:17.154
But you won't hear anyone laughing at them anymore!
